(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Early childhood represents a critical developmental window for obesity) Tj
T*
(prevention. Schools and family environments shape dietary and activity patterns but lack) Tj
T*
(effective evidence-based tools. We describe a protocol for a cluster-randomized trial of a) Tj
T*
(multi-level digital health intervention targeting preschool-aged children. Methods:) Tj
T*
(Fourteen kindergartens in an urban Chinese region will be randomly assigned to) Tj
T*
(intervention \(7 sites\) or control \(7 sites\). Approximately 980 children aged 4-6 years) Tj
T*
(will be enrolled from middle and senior kindergarten classrooms. The intervention) Tj
T*
(comprises a cloud-based health platform providing real-time activity tracking,) Tj
T*
(personalized dietary recommendations, and structured family education sessions.) Tj
T*
(Implementation spans 9 months, with extended 12-month post-intervention surveillance. Data) Tj
T*
(collection occurs at baseline, 4 months, 9 months, and 21 months. Primary outcome:) Tj
T*
(between-group change in BMI z-score at the 9-month endpoint. Secondary outcomes:) Tj
T*
(anthropometric measures, dietary quality, assessed activity levels, cost-effectiveness.) Tj
T*
(Fidelity and engagement metrics will be tracked throughout. Process evaluation will) Tj
T*
(identify implementation barriers and facilitators.) Tj
